## Cells & Microscopy
@ Cell membrane | Cell wall | Nucleus | Ribosome
Which structure regulates cellular entry and exit?|0|Its selective permeability regulates exchange.
Which rigid outer structure supports plant cells?|1|The cellulose wall adds support.
Which compartment contains most eukaryotic DNA?|2|Nuclear chromosomes contain most of it.
Which structure synthesizes proteins?|3|Ribosomes translate messenger RNA.
@ Mitochondrion | Chloroplast | Vacuole | Cytoplasm
Which organelle carries out major aerobic ATP-producing reactions?|0|Aerobic respiration supplies ATP.
Where does photosynthesis occur in a leaf cell?|1|Chloroplasts contain photosynthetic machinery.
Which large plant compartment helps maintain turgor?|2|Its water content contributes pressure.
What surrounds organelles inside the membrane?|3|Cytoplasm includes the intracellular material outside the nucleus.
@ 40× | 100× | 400× | 1000×
A 10× eyepiece and 4× objective give what total magnification?|0|Multiply eyepiece and objective powers.
@ 100 μm | 10 μm | 1 μm | 1000 μm
A cell image is 10 mm long at 100× magnification. What is the actual cell length?|0|10 mm divided by 100 is 0.1 mm, or 100 μm.
@ 400 μm | 40 μm | 4 μm | 4000 μm
Four cells, each 100 μm wide, fit edge to edge across a circular field diameter. Estimate that diameter.|0|Four widths of 100 μm give 400 μm.
@ 1000 μm | 100 μm | 10 μm | 10000 μm
A microscope scale bar represents 1 mm. How many micrometres does it represent?|0|One millimetre equals 1000 micrometres.
@ Begin with low power | Use fine focus at high power | Add a suitable stain | Prepare a thin specimen
Which approach helps locate a specimen initially?|0|Low power offers a wider field.
Which adjustment minimizes large lens movement near a slide at high power?|1|Fine focus makes small adjustments.
Transparent cells have poor contrast. Which preparation can improve visibility?|2|A suitable stain differentiates structures.
Overlapping tissue obscures cells. Which preparation helps transmit light?|3|A thin section reduces overlap.
@ Resolution differs from magnification | Smaller field of view | Plant cells are eukaryotic | Absence in one image is not proof of absence
An enlarged image remains blurry and cannot separate adjacent structures. What distinction matters?|0|Making an image larger does not necessarily reveal more detail.
A pupil changes to a higher-power objective. Why are fewer cells visible at once?|1|Higher magnification typically narrows the viewed area.
A specimen has a nucleus and cellulose wall. Which classification fits?|2|Both features characterize typical plant cells.
No mitochondria are visible in a basic light-microscope image. What is the cautious inference?|3|Preparation, contrast and resolution limit what is visible.
## Genetics & Heredity
@ Gene | Allele | Genotype | Phenotype
What is a hereditary DNA unit contributing to a functional product?|0|Genes encode functional products.
What is a variant form of a gene?|1|Alternative forms are alleles.
What describes an organism's allele combination?|2|Genotype records its genetic constitution.
What describes an observable expressed trait?|3|Phenotype is the expressed characteristic.
@ AA | Aa | aa | Gamete
Which is homozygous dominant in the A/a model?|0|Both alleles are dominant.
Which is heterozygous in the A/a model?|1|The alleles differ.
Which is homozygous recessive in the A/a model?|2|Both alleles are recessive.
Which reproductive cell normally carries one allele per locus?|3|A gamete is haploid.
@ 0 | 1/4 | 1/2 | 1
Two homozygous dominant parents (AA) reproduce. What fraction of their offspring will be aa?|0|Neither parent contributes a.
In a cross between two heterozygotes, what fraction is expected to be homozygous recessive?|1|Only one of four equally likely combinations is aa.
A heterozygote is crossed with an aa parent. What proportion of offspring is predicted to be aa?|2|Half the gametes from Aa carry a.
Both parents are homozygous recessive. What is the probability that their child also has genotype aa?|3|Both parents contribute only a.
@ Meiosis | Fertilization | Mutation | DNA replication
Which process reduces chromosome number for gametes?|0|Meiosis forms haploid cells.
Which event combines two gametes?|1|Their union restores the diploid number.
Which term means a change in DNA sequence?|2|Mutation changes genetic information.
Which process copies DNA before division?|3|Replication duplicates DNA molecules.
@ Probability is not a guaranteed small-sample ratio | Environment can affect phenotype | Dominance does not imply high frequency | Sex-linked inheritance can differ by sex
Four offspring from Aa × Aa all show the dominant phenotype. Why is this possible?|0|Random small samples can depart from expected ratios.
Genetically similar plants grow differently under different nutrition. What explains this?|1|Expression reflects environmental as well as genetic factors.
An allele is dominant but rare. Is this contradictory?|2|Expression relationships and population frequency are distinct.
An X-linked recessive trait is expressed more often in XY individuals. What explains this?|3|They usually have only one copy of the X-linked locus.
## Evolution & Biotechnology
@ Natural selection | Mutation | Genetic drift | Gene flow
Which process favors heritable variants that improve reproductive success?|0|Differential reproduction changes variant frequencies.
Which process introduces new DNA sequence variants?|1|Sequence changes can create new alleles.
Which process changes allele frequencies through chance sampling?|2|Random effects matter especially in small populations.
Which process transfers alleles between populations through reproduction?|3|Migration followed by reproduction can move alleles.
@ Homologous structures | Analogous structures | Vestigial structures | Fossils
Which structures share evolutionary origin despite different functions?|0|Shared ancestry explains the structural correspondence.
Bird wings and insect wings share flight function but arose differently. Which description fits?|1|Similar function evolved independently.
Reduced structures inherited from ancestors illustrate what?|2|Their ancestral function may be diminished.
Preserved traces of ancient organisms are what?|3|Fossils provide evidence of past life.
@ Artificial selection | Selective pressure | Common ancestry | Adaptation
Humans breed crops for preferred traits. What process is this?|0|Humans choose reproductive traits.
An antibiotic changes which bacteria survive. What environmental role does it play?|1|It changes differential survival conditions.
Shared inherited molecular similarities can support what?|2|Descent can explain shared features.
A heritable feature improves reproductive success in its environment. What is it?|3|An adaptation is advantageous in a particular setting.
@ PCR | Gel electrophoresis | Genetic engineering | DNA sequencing
Which method amplifies a selected DNA region?|0|PCR produces many copies.
Which method separates DNA fragments by their migration through a gel?|1|Fragment size influences migration.
Introducing a chosen genetic change using biotechnology is what?|2|Genetic engineering modifies genetic material deliberately.
Which method determines the order of DNA bases?|3|Sequencing reads nucleotide order.
@ Individuals do not evolve by need alone | Resistance can pre-exist treatment | A population bottleneck reduces diversity | Benefits and risks require case-specific evaluation
Why is 'a giraffe stretched, so its offspring inherited a longer neck' inadequate as a natural-selection explanation?|0|Selection concerns inherited population variation across generations.
Why can antibiotic-resistant bacteria become common during treatment?|1|Resistant variants can survive and reproduce disproportionately.
A disaster leaves very few random survivors. What genetic risk arises?|2|Random loss can remove alleles.
Is every genetically modified product automatically safe or automatically harmful?|3|The trait, exposure and evidence must be assessed.
## Matter & Atomic Structure
@ Proton | Electron | Neutron | Nucleus
Which particle carries positive charge?|0|Protons have positive elementary charge.
Which particle carries negative charge?|1|Electrons have negative elementary charge.
Which particle is electrically neutral?|2|Neutrons have no net charge.
Where are protons and neutrons located?|3|They occupy the atomic nucleus.
@ Atomic number | Mass number | Isotope | Ion
What equals the number of protons?|0|Proton number identifies the element.
What equals protons plus neutrons?|1|Mass number counts nucleons.
Atoms of one element with different neutron counts are what?|2|Isotopes share proton number.
An atom with net electrical charge is what?|3|Electron imbalance produces an ion.
@ Element | Compound | Homogeneous mixture | Heterogeneous mixture
A pure substance containing only one type of atom is what?|0|One element has a characteristic proton number.
A pure substance with chemically combined elements in fixed ratio is what?|1|A compound has definite composition.
Saltwater uniformly mixed at the observed scale is what?|2|A solution is homogeneous.
Sand mixed with water and visibly settling is what?|3|Its composition is not uniform.
@ Melting | Condensation | Evaporation | Sublimation
Solid ice changes into liquid water. Name the process.|0|Solid becomes liquid.
Water vapor forms liquid droplets. Name the process.|1|Gas becomes liquid.
Liquid water gradually becomes vapor at its surface. Name the process.|2|Surface molecules escape into the gas phase.
Dry ice changes directly from solid to gas. Name the process.|3|There is no intermediate liquid phase.
@ 6 | 8 | 12 | 18
A carbon atom has atomic number 6. Proton count?|0|Atomic number equals protons.
An atom has 6 protons and mass number 14. Neutron count?|1|14 minus 6 equals 8.
An atom has 6 protons and 6 neutrons. Mass number?|2|Add its nucleons.
A neutral argon atom has atomic number 18. Electron count?|3|Neutrality requires equal protons and electrons.
## Chemical Bonding
@ Ionic bond | Covalent bond | Metallic bond | Hydrogen bond
Which interaction holds oppositely charged ions in a lattice?|0|Electrostatic attraction binds the ions.
Which bond involves shared electron pairs between atoms?|1|Covalent bonds share electrons.
Which bonding model includes delocalized electrons among metal atoms?|2|Mobile electrons help bind the metal.
Which interaction can occur between water molecules?|3|A hydrogen associated with an electronegative atom interacts with another molecule.
@ Cation | Anion | Neutral atom | Molecule
An atom loses an electron. What charged species results?|0|Electron loss makes net positive charge.
An atom gains an electron. What charged species results?|1|Electron gain makes net negative charge.
Equal proton and electron counts describe what?|2|Charges cancel.
A discrete group of covalently bonded atoms is what?|3|A molecule is a bonded atomic group.
@ NaCl | MgCl₂ | Na₂O | CaO
Which neutral formula balances Na⁺ and Cl⁻?|0|The charges balance one to one.
Which formula balances Mg²⁺ and Cl⁻?|1|Two chloride ions balance one magnesium ion.
Which formula balances Na⁺ and O²⁻?|2|Two sodium ions balance oxide.
Which formula balances Ca²⁺ and O²⁻?|3|Equal opposite charges give a one-to-one ratio.
@ Polar covalent | Nonpolar covalent | Ionic | Metallic
A bond shares electrons unequally. Which description fits?|0|Unequal attraction creates partial charges.
Two identical atoms share a bonding pair equally. Which description fits?|1|There is no electronegativity difference.
Electron transfer produces oppositely charged ions. Which bond type follows?|2|The resulting ions attract electrostatically.
A material conducts through delocalized electrons in its solid structure. Which model fits?|3|Mobile electrons characterize metals.
@ Molecular shape matters | Mobile ions are needed for ionic conduction | Bond polarity is not the same as molecular polarity | Conservation of charge determines formula
Why is water polar although both O–H bonds belong to one molecule?|0|Its bent shape prevents bond dipoles from canceling.
Why does molten sodium chloride conduct better than its solid lattice?|1|Its ions can move in the melt.
Why can a symmetric molecule have polar bonds but zero net dipole?|2|Oppositely directed bond dipoles can cancel.
Why is MgCl not the neutral formula for Mg²⁺ and Cl⁻?|3|One chloride cannot balance a +2 charge.
## Chemical Reactions & Stoichiometry
@ Reactant | Product | Coefficient | Catalyst
What is a starting substance in a chemical reaction?|0|Reactants are consumed or transformed.
What is a substance formed by a reaction?|1|Products appear after the reaction arrow.
What multiplies a complete formula in a balanced equation?|2|A coefficient counts formula units or molecules.
What speeds a reaction without being consumed overall?|3|A catalyst provides an alternative pathway.
@ Conservation of atoms | Conservation of charge | Mole ratio | Limiting reactant
Why balance each element in an equation?|0|Ordinary chemical reactions rearrange atoms.
Why must ionic equations balance net electrical charge?|1|Charge is conserved.
What do balanced coefficients provide for reacting amounts in moles?|2|They express stoichiometric proportions.
Which reactant is consumed first and limits product yield?|3|The limiting reactant caps possible product.
@ 2 | 1 | 3 | 4
For 2H₂ + O₂ → 2H₂O, moles of water per mole of O₂?|0|The coefficients give a 2:1 ratio.
In 2H₂ + O₂ → 2H₂O, how many moles of oxygen react completely with 2 mol hydrogen?|1|One mole oxygen reacts with two hydrogen.
Balance N₂ + H₂ → NH₃. Coefficient of H₂ in the smallest whole-number equation?|2|N₂ + 3H₂ → 2NH₃.
For CH₄ + 2O₂ → CO₂ + 2H₂O, oxygen moles needed for 2 mol methane?|3|Double the oxygen coefficient.
@ Precipitation | Combustion | Acid–base neutralization | Decomposition
Two solutions form an insoluble solid. What process occurred?|0|A precipitate is a newly formed insoluble solid.
A fuel reacts rapidly with oxygen and releases heat. Which process fits?|1|This is characteristic combustion.
An acid reacts with a base to form salt and water. Which type fits?|2|The process neutralizes acid and base equivalents.
One compound breaks into simpler substances. Which type fits?|3|Decomposition splits a reactant.
@ Change coefficients, not subscripts | Account for escaping gas | Calculate from the limiting reactant | Distinguish actual from theoretical yield
Why is balancing water formation by replacing H₂O with H₂O₂ invalid?|0|Changing a subscript changes the compound.
An open reacting flask loses measured mass while bubbling. What is needed to assess conservation?|1|Material left the measured system.
Two reactants are not in exact stoichiometric proportion. How is maximum yield determined?|2|The first exhausted reactant limits output.
A reaction yields less collected product than predicted. What distinction is essential?|3|Theoretical yield assumes ideal conversion and recovery.
## Electricity & Magnetism
@ Current | Voltage | Resistance | Charge
What measures rate of charge flow?|0|Current is charge per unit time.
What is electric potential difference?|1|Voltage is energy transferred per unit charge.
What quantifies opposition to current flow?|2|Resistance relates voltage to current.
What conserved property has positive and negative forms?|3|Electric charge underlies electric interactions.
@ Ampere | Volt | Ohm | Watt
What is the SI unit of current?|0|Current is measured in amperes.
What is the unit of potential difference?|1|Potential difference is measured in volts.
What is the unit of resistance?|2|Resistance is measured in ohms.
What is the unit of electrical power?|3|Power is measured in watts.
@ 2 A | 3 A | 6 A | 0.5 A
A 6 V source acts across 3 Ω. Current?|0|I = V/R = 2.
A 12 V source acts across 4 Ω. Current?|1|12 divided by 4 equals 3.
A 12 V source acts across 2 Ω. Current?|2|12 divided by 2 equals 6.
An ohmic component has resistance 6 Ω. Under a potential difference of 3 V, how much charge flows per second?|3|3 divided by 6 equals 0.5.
@ Series circuit | Parallel circuit | Electromagnet | Open circuit
Components share one unbranched path. Identify the circuit.|0|The same current passes through each component.
Components connect across the same two nodes. Identify the circuit.|1|Each branch has the same potential difference.
A current-carrying coil produces a magnetic field. What device principle is this?|2|Electric current creates the magnetic field.
A conducting path is interrupted. Identify its condition.|3|The interruption prevents continuous current in the path.
@ Like magnetic poles repel | Current can produce magnetic effects | Resistance and current depend on conditions | Isolate power before electrical work
Two north poles are brought together. What interaction is expected?|0|Like poles repel.
A compass deflects near a current-carrying wire. What does this demonstrate?|1|A current is associated with a magnetic field.
Why is doubling voltage not sufficient to guarantee exactly double current in every device?|2|Resistance need not remain constant.
Why is a disconnected supply essential before inspecting damaged equipment?|3|Removing hazardous energy reduces shock risk.
## Waves, Light & Optics
@ Frequency | Wavelength | Amplitude | Period
What counts oscillations per second?|0|Frequency measures cycles per unit time.
What is the distance between successive corresponding wave points?|1|For example, between neighboring crests.
What is maximum displacement from equilibrium?|2|Amplitude measures displacement magnitude.
What is time per complete oscillation?|3|Period is the duration of one cycle.
@ Transverse | Longitudinal | Electromagnetic | Mechanical
Displacement is perpendicular to propagation. Classify the wave.|0|Transverse motion is across the travel direction.
Displacement is parallel to propagation. Classify the wave.|1|Longitudinal waves have compressions and rarefactions.
Which wave type can travel through vacuum?|2|Electromagnetic waves require no material medium.
Which class requires a material medium?|3|Mechanical waves propagate disturbances in matter.
@ 10 m/s | 20 m/s | 5 Hz | 0.2 s
A wave has f = 5 Hz and λ = 2 m. Speed?|0|v = fλ = 10.
Successive wave crests are separated by 5 m, and four pass a fixed point each second. How fast does the pattern travel?|1|4 × 5 = 20.
Twenty-five oscillations occur in 5 s. Frequency?|2|25 divided by 5 is 5.
A wave has frequency 5 Hz. Period?|3|T = 1/f = 0.2.
@ Reflection | Refraction | Diffraction | Interference
A light ray returns from a mirror. Name the phenomenon.|0|Reflection returns light into its incident medium.
A ray changes direction entering another medium obliquely. Name the phenomenon.|1|A change in wave speed can bend the ray.
Waves spread around an opening. Name the phenomenon.|2|Diffraction occurs near edges and apertures.
Overlapping waves produce reinforcement and cancellation. Name the phenomenon.|3|Their displacements superpose.
@ Angles are measured from the normal | Frequency stays fixed across a stationary boundary | Medium particles need not travel with the wave | Sound needs a medium
Why is a ray 30° from a mirror surface 60° from the normal?|0|The surface and normal are perpendicular.
Light enters glass from air. Which frequency principle applies?|1|The source frequency is maintained while speed and wavelength change.
Why does a floating marker mainly oscillate as a wave passes?|2|The disturbance transports energy without equivalent bulk travel.
Why cannot a bell be heard through an ideal vacuum surrounding it?|3|There is no material path for sound propagation.
## Earth Systems & Geological Processes
@ Atmosphere | Hydrosphere | Geosphere | Biosphere
Which system includes Earth's surrounding gases?|0|The atmosphere is the gaseous envelope.
Which system includes oceans and groundwater?|1|The hydrosphere includes Earth's water.
Which system includes rocks and the solid Earth?|2|The geosphere comprises solid Earth materials.
Which system includes living organisms?|3|The biosphere includes life.
@ Weathering | Erosion | Deposition | Compaction
Rock breaks down in place. Name the process.|0|Weathering does not require transport.
A river carries sediment downstream. Name the process.|1|Erosion removes and transports material.
Transported sediment settles. Name the process.|2|Deposition lays sediment down.
Buried sediment becomes more tightly packed. Name the process.|3|Pressure reduces pore spaces.
@ Igneous | Sedimentary | Metamorphic | Magma
Which rock forms when molten material solidifies?|0|Cooling produces igneous rock.
Which rock can form through lithification of sediments?|1|Sediments become sedimentary rock.
Which rock changes under heat and pressure without complete melting?|2|Metamorphism alters existing rock.
What is molten rock beneath the surface?|3|Below-ground melt is magma.
@ Evaporation | Condensation | Infiltration | Transpiration
Liquid surface water becomes vapor. Name the process.|0|Water enters the gas phase.
Vapor becomes cloud droplets. Name the process.|1|Cooling can form liquid droplets.
Water enters soil from the surface. Name the process.|2|Infiltration supplies subsurface water.
Plants release water vapor through leaves. Name the process.|3|Transpiration links vegetation with atmospheric water.
@ Interacting Earth systems | Soil saturation and gravity | Evidence across several processes | Human modification of runoff
A volcanic eruption affects air, rivers and forests. What principle is illustrated?|0|Earth systems exchange matter and energy.
A prolonged storm precedes slope failure. Which mechanism should be examined?|1|Water can weaken a slope while gravity drives failure.
Why does finding rounded pebbles alone not reconstruct an entire landscape's history?|2|Several processes and later changes may be involved.
Impermeable urban paving increases flood peaks. Which influence is demonstrated?|3|Reduced infiltration can increase rapid surface flow.
## Plate Tectonics
@ Divergent boundary | Convergent boundary | Transform boundary | Plate interior
Where do plates move apart?|0|Divergence separates plates.
Where do plates move toward each other?|1|Convergence can produce collision or subduction.
Where do plates slide laterally past each other?|2|Transform motion is mainly horizontal.
Where is an intraplate earthquake located relative to plate edges?|3|It occurs away from a boundary.
@ Subduction | Seafloor spreading | Continental collision | Fault slip
One plate descends beneath another. Name the process.|0|Dense lithosphere can sink at a convergent boundary.
New oceanic crust forms at a ridge. Name the process.|1|Spreading creates crust as plates separate.
Two buoyant continental masses converge and thicken crust. Name the process.|2|Collision can build mountain belts.
Stored elastic strain is suddenly released along a fracture. Name the motion.|3|Fault slip can generate an earthquake.
@ Lithosphere | Asthenosphere | Mantle convection | Magnetic stripes
Which relatively rigid layer forms tectonic plates?|0|It includes crust and uppermost mantle.
Which weaker mantle region permits slow deformation beneath plates?|1|The asthenosphere can deform over long times.
Which process involves heat-driven circulation within Earth's mantle?|2|Convection transfers heat by material motion.
Which seafloor evidence records reversals symmetrically around ridges?|3|Magnetic patterns support seafloor spreading.
@ Earthquake distribution | Matching fossils | Ocean-floor age | Measured plate motion
A belt of epicenters outlines plate margins. Which evidence is being used?|0|Seismicity helps locate active boundaries.
Related ancient fossils occur on widely separated continents. Which evidence supports earlier connection?|1|Biogeographic matches can support past continental proximity.
Crust becomes older away from a spreading ridge. Which evidence is being used?|2|New crust forms near the ridge.
Repeated satellite positions show continents shifting millimeters yearly. Which evidence is direct?|3|Geodesy measures present motion.
@ Boundaries differ in their hazards | Plates move over geologic time | A hotspot need not be a plate boundary | Local risk also depends on vulnerability
Why should a transform boundary not be assumed to produce the same volcanism as a subduction zone?|0|Different motions and melting conditions produce different hazards.
Why can continents have very different ancient arrangements?|1|Persistent motion accumulates across millions of years.
An island chain forms over a relatively persistent mantle source beneath a moving plate. What distinction matters?|2|Hotspot volcanism can occur within a plate.
Two towns equally near a fault suffer different losses. What else must be evaluated?|3|Construction, exposure and ground conditions affect damage.
